is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: mswitch1
diagnosis_text:
- Affected component: VLAN 10 tagging on the trunk between access switch switch1 and distribution switch mswitch1. Specifically, the VLAN 10 subinterface on mswitch1 (eth0.10) is down.
- Mechanism: PCs on VLAN 10 (pc4, pc5) can talk to each other locally on switch1, proving the access VLAN and local bridging are fine. However, reaching the gateway 172.16.10.1 requires traversing the trunk to mswitch1 and then to router0. With eth0.10 down on mswitch1, VLAN 10 traffic from switch1 cannot enter mswitch1, so ARP/gateway traffic fails and pings return “Destination Host Unreachable.”
- Localization: On switch1, br_vlan10 and eth0.10 are UP and members of br_vlan10 (evidence: switch1 ip addr shows eth0.10 state UP/master br_vlan10). On router0, the gateway interface eth0.10 is UP with 172.16.10.1/24. On mswitch1, eth4.10 (toward router0) is UP, but eth0.10 (toward switch1) is DOWN (mswitch1 ip addr shows “eth0.10 … state DOWN”), while eth0.20 is UP—explaining why VLAN 20 (laptop0) is unaffected.
- Root cause: The mswitch1 VLAN 10 subinterface (eth0.10) on the trunk to switch1 is administratively down, breaking the VLAN 10 L2 path from switch1 to the gateway.
- Corrective fix: Bring up and permit VLAN 10 on the mswitch1 trunk toward switch1: ip link set dev eth0.10 up and ensure it remains a member of br_vlan10. Verify eth0.10 shows LOWER_UP and that br_vlan10 includes eth0.10; then re-test pc4/pc5 to 172.16.10.1.

=== END ===